  "profile": {
    "id": "griffiths_squibb_fig6",
    "title": {
      "en": "#2 Prof. Griffiths (G3ZIL) et al. RX Comparison: Day/Night SNR Reversal: Low Dipole vs Vertical"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Publication: Griffiths, G., & Squibb, N. J. (2017). Improving HF Band SNR. Practical Wireless, October 2017, pp. 23–26](https://www.wsprnet.org/drupal/sites/wsprnet.org/files/G3ZIL%20G4HZX%20WSPR%20Improving%20HF%20SNR-print.pdf)\n\nThis demo extends the first Griffiths and Squibb demo, moving from the month-long comparison in Figure 3 to the daily pattern in **Figure 6**. It focuses on **5–7 April 2017**, comparing the same two receiving stations on 40 m: **G3ZIL, using a low horizontal dipole**, and **G4HZX, using a vertical antenna without radials**.\n\n[**Figure 6 comparison (PDF)**](app/static/reference_figures/griffiths_fig6_paper_v1/WSPRadar_Demo_Griffiths_Figure6.pdf): Compare the original published figure with a reconstruction and the WSPRadar chart.\n\nWSPRadar matches reports of the same transmitting station from the same two-minute WSPR cycle. Their signal-to-noise ratio difference is **ΔSNR = G3ZIL − G4HZX**: positive values favour G3ZIL, negative values favour G4HZX, and 0 dB means equal reported SNR.\n\nGo to **Segment Inspector → Temporal Evidence**, and explore **Δ SNR by UTC Hour (1 h bins)**. This chart combines observations from the same hour on all three dates — for example, reports between 07:00 and 08:00 UTC appear together, regardless of their date. This is called *UTC-hour folding* and helps reveal a daily pattern.\n\nLook for G4HZX’s advantage in the early hours: the hourly medians are about **−1 to −3 dB at 01–04 UTC**. They become positive from **05 UTC**, reaching **+5 to +8 dB at 07–20 UTC**. The markers show hourly medians, the shaded band shows the middle 50% of observations, and the colours show where observations are concentrated.\n\nCompare this with **Δ SNR over Time** to see how the pattern differs between the individual dates. Use the map and **Segment Inspector** to explore different directions and distances, or select a transmitting station in **Station Insights** to examine one radio path.\n\nThese are patterns in the combined observations, not a result shared by every transmission. As in the first demo, the comparison includes antenna, location, equipment and local-noise effects; it does not isolate their causes."
    }
  },
